course: dictionary
lesson: 13068
title: Fend: look after yourself and push away
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Fend is a verb with two main meanings. Use the words around it to work out which one you need. «The fox cub had to fend for itself after its mother went away.» The cub has no one to help it, so fend for yourself must mean to look after yourself without help.
explain: Check your meaning by swapping it into the sentence. «The fox cub had to look after itself after its mother went away.» It still makes sense, so your meaning fits. Now try the other meaning: «Sam used his umbrella to fend off the angry seagull.» Fend off here means push it away, because the umbrella is used to keep the gull back.
explain: Fend is also a rugby word. A fend is when a player pushes a tackler away with the arm. It has the same idea as fend off: keeping something away from you.
fact: Fend is a verb: fend, fends, fended, fending.
fact: «Fend for yourself» means to look after yourself without help.
fact: «Fend off» means to push something away or defend yourself against it.
fact: «Fend off» can also mean to keep away questions, as in «She fended off all the questions about her surprise.»
fact: In rugby, a fend is pushing a tackler away with your arm.
exercise: «The fox cub had to fend for itself.» What does fend for itself mean? | Hide in a warm den all day | Dig a hole to sleep in | *Look after itself without help | Wait for its mother to come back
exercise: Sam used his umbrella to fend off the seagull. What does fend off mean here? | *Push the seagull away | Give the seagull a present | Sing a song to the seagull | Fall over in the rain
exercise: A rugby player uses his arm to fend off a tackler. What is a fend? | A pass to a friend behind | A long run to the try line | A kick over the top of the posts | *Pushing a tackler away with your arm
test: «The little bird had to fend for itself when its parents flew south.» What does fend for itself mean? | Stay in its nest all day | Ask its family to feed it | Run away from home | *Look after itself without help
test: «She fended off all the questions about her surprise party.» What does fended off mean? | Asked even more questions | Answered every question quickly | *Pushed the questions away and kept the surprise | Wrote the questions down
exam: «The puppy tried to fend off the big dog, but it was too small.» What does fend off mean here? | *Push the big dog away | Run to the big dog for a cuddle | Fall asleep beside the big dog | Wag its tail at the big dog
